#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151863 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "e49f7799-2724-5fe0-999f-bfa9ab5ce610" :question/math-academy-id "e-6244" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/4faf932e63a57d2fe53fb3942cfe21c65d0d2a88692c665b2706bd68164855c9.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum,\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/26dc2a5af4c82657d9ec6fa331d4f5bc7575367bd4e3a00a5929b4ecaffe4477.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6d34737c12c35f6faec5caf37be500ba16c3f7cb18bf5ae660dc6d54182b66da.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=5 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,2)=6 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,3)=7 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(2,3)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =5+6+7+8=26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151864 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "1ac785c8-3d14-5968-94d3-6f4588f08d91" :question/math-academy-id "e-6245" :question/problem "Let $R = [1,5] \\times [2,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,3,5},\\,{P}_{2}={2,4,6},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/5133b73c3707b87725baeee9a34a64898d33056dab9d7d32ac8cc24dbe772321.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ regions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{21}+4\\cdot {M}_{12}+4\\cdot {M}_{22} \\\\  & =4({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/5b711e2c33e8966dfc9f4274a8f556f712d51a450bbcd0cadb68771fd050d304.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/ad83cf465252b1f880757405ca3ff9ab5e1b9b1e50d712bd3b81305b7973af27.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=60 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(3,2)=52 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,4)=48 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(3,4)=40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4(60+52+48+40)=800.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151865 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/id #uuid "14e77998-b24b-56c1-8901-dc94d3ef98a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6246" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/33269c79413f4435d074d90cd727f2a53e7b9831300bfcaf0b5a7122df764080.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Find an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $2$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU = M_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = 8\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =8\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{21} \\\\  & =8{M}_{11}+4{M}_{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97d415b0-6c5c-53d2-9d2c-4d6dd0353cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M_{11} \\cdot M_{21} \\cdot M_{12} \\cdot M_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148855} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cb6ea2-bdd6-547b-a4bb-ede86c15e21f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148856} {:answer/id #uuid "ac617ab9-1584-56b9-ab51-0541d60e2c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M_{11} - M_{21} + M_{12} - M_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148857} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc96657-5863-5b52-bf69-d9a6de137de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148858} {:answer/id #uuid "de8afcbf-c1bb-537e-9566-e29997041eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2M_{11} + M_{21} + 2M_{12} + M_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de8afcbf-c1bb-537e-9566-e29997041eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2M_{11} + M_{21} + 2M_{12} + M_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148859} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef0062db-958f-5d6c-a60a-f5c94523fcb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bab7c56-0d70-56a8-b7e8-20549b73d742" :question/math-academy-id "q-122847" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/c28d3a6098da6c8be022974e96b5242e3bed58ef1347d92571b49c7b521a3b0d.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=\\text{Area}({R}_{22})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =2\\cdot {M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2\\cdot {M}_{12}+{M}_{22} \\\\  & =2{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64c68731-d6de-508d-9b6c-62d9edbc2fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{M}_{11}+2{M}_{21}+4{M}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375508} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc38173-7018-5176-aae9-3fec91590943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{21}\\cdot {M}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375509} {:answer/id #uuid "59cd0e0e-bd7f-5623-9ddb-870eac2d731e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+3{M}_{21}+2{M}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375510} {:answer/id #uuid "f2613587-10c3-52c5-9bb6-9421e3e6e0c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375511} {:answer/id #uuid "f1315643-ed65-5213-b969-a4e89517d523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2{M}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64c68731-d6de-508d-9b6c-62d9edbc2fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{M}_{11}+2{M}_{21}+4{M}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375508} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccb9aac3-023f-5d25-bdd6-f731e85c2043" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa022868-d2a1-543e-8a08-e76c74c86a57" :question/math-academy-id "q-123049" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/76654da8bebc4a156a55bcb6cc7411f4977dfa14634fbceb6e1a7f8e32b2d5b0.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=6, \\\\ \\text{Area}({R}_{21})=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{31})=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =6\\cdot {M}_{11}+2\\cdot {M}_{21}+4\\cdot {M}_{31} \\\\  & =6{M}_{11}+2{M}_{21}+4{M}_{31}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4197d6a8-a79a-5820-b4df-1abdee126a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375515} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a957d6-7e6b-54e6-b206-89dba371f47a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{M}_{11}+{M}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375516} {:answer/id #uuid "5a500cfd-fe4e-5696-a49a-68ab0ced6ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+3{M}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375517} {:answer/id #uuid "e52bdb81-e811-5ed4-ad54-b133904035cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375518} {:answer/id #uuid "91fd11fe-6e3d-5227-838b-0c76f012ebba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+{M}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a500cfd-fe4e-5696-a49a-68ab0ced6ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+3{M}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375517} :answer-field/id #uuid "817f21d7-bbfa-5ee8-8fba-7adb3f49a63d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4d51159-1338-5fc9-a5d2-24d45f079eee" :question/math-academy-id "q-123050" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/f77ec60f3208cd221570e30ed8f4c1684607dc54567435071dd34ccf76ea5227.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $2$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & ={M}_{11}+3\\cdot {M}_{12} \\\\  & ={M}_{11}+3{M}_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68932671-b578-5511-9072-5a93bfe54efc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{12}\\cdot {M}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375522} {:answer/id #uuid "67b20bf9-a92b-5a76-87cf-505c254eebab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375523} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9e9070-ae67-5179-a95c-19d7328914d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+2{M}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375524} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6cac92-0b42-547c-a4ee-b2e6dff6fd5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375525} {:answer/id #uuid "52c80982-ba6f-5816-94ed-c29ea6e84a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca6cac92-0b42-547c-a4ee-b2e6dff6fd5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375525} :answer-field/id #uuid "199ca536-8869-53dc-a2ac-5b195bb772d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45c919fc-f012-5098-a177-120291fb4f75" :question/math-academy-id "q-123051" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/c11167f3e67f712b894cc56aef96897e5a75b1a3e0d1e3ca214f2fd48a70febb.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{13})=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & ={M}_{11}+2\\cdot {M}_{12}+{M}_{13} \\\\  & ={M}_{11}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa8da8f-00bb-5663-b578-99086398a1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Upper Bound in Terms of Maximum Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c3675c4-f868-515a-8eff-8b5cba5d07a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{M}_{11}+10{M}_{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375529} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8c2c3b-08cf-5d37-945d-b944dcf5f1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{M}_{11}+2{M}_{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375530} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a106c5-f3b8-5178-b18c-f3a1d7d40e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+5{M}_{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375531} {:answer/id #uuid "36a831ed-1c52-532b-94d5-0171b12a603f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375532} {:answer/id #uuid "443bc837-3151-567a-8574-996a2c74437b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c3675c4-f868-515a-8eff-8b5cba5d07a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{M}_{11}+10{M}_{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375529} :answer-field/id #uuid "840f6e9f-cfe7-5fe4-91f4-4781f047355b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5a2285c-007a-5c4d-b1d4-af08d8caa89a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123054" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/0b41aab14e66fe196a2720e65d4f814da55acfdb0c7b8338ff9e44acfe808785.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $2$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =2\\cdot {M}_{11}+10\\cdot {M}_{21} \\\\  & =2{M}_{11}+10{M}_{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b17d64e-c3a2-5fa0-9f7d-e604d552cb36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375536} {:answer/id #uuid "e5331e1c-d894-5a06-816c-9fa44c4f0e88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375537} {:answer/id #uuid "05cff596-698d-5e3e-a915-3be53d63b782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375538} {:answer/id #uuid "be45a78b-75dd-5770-a53b-23873c95e773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "450" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375539} {:answer/id #uuid "25ec5b65-0ac2-59e4-b883-550524b91836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b17d64e-c3a2-5fa0-9f7d-e604d552cb36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375536} :answer-field/id #uuid "884dab17-71e2-5757-adb9-19776e524395" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "173dae7c-03a9-58e0-bb98-2869b614756e" :question/math-academy-id "q-123132" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,7]\\times [1,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=xy.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,3,5,7},\\,{P}_{2}={1,6},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/68270610704b1ef132469193e7948f54e548fa22223b6fc49b693753f998f874.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=10$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =10\\cdot {M}_{11}+10\\cdot {M}_{21}+10\\cdot {M}_{31} \\\\  & =10({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=xy$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=xy$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/7611c64cc460db725893156f6043644b34962a04f2aacef0b19cf99c5769cda3.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/75f378700ce6593a7ed9577b34e093cbbfe702e9512e9962e0d6f7f681694842.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(3,6)=18 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(5,6)=30 \\\\ {M}_{31} & =f(7,6)=42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =10(18+30+42) \\\\  & =900.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d1d6033-7bb0-5907-9493-f7f55396bb1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375543} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1b060d-1ac3-5bc8-926c-b10b8114853c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375544} {:answer/id #uuid "b37f1b8f-6b7a-50a6-bd26-0ddc41840d70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375545} {:answer/id #uuid "d3709103-3041-578b-a299-44a8d659e08e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375546} {:answer/id #uuid "abfdb549-b090-5c82-86bc-be36173d8c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3709103-3041-578b-a299-44a8d659e08e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375546} :answer-field/id #uuid "38f6fe25-5c91-5f50-8899-8729f70e33ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54485fed-7aa4-5fa8-89fb-f4c092b8268e" :question/math-academy-id "q-123233" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,1]\\times [0,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=8-4x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6fffcb4b3db9eab7d8bd7fbd253ed533ee95bc22eb5291b8b58041091e83b241.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=8-4x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=8-4x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/eda5e419fe95a7176f241c64f7d2452267fc2bc87d1e351b990e24665e9f0f94.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/cd1793250b5f95950acd195afc4d72ff94ac4eaee4719ad3e4064b8e9d5b8af6.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(0,0)=8 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(0,1)=7 \\\\ {M}_{13} & =f(0,2)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =8+7+6 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e52a7d5-7212-505d-a4ad-a9e51e77aaac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "186" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375550} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b609d0-f1e9-5e0b-bdd0-bdb9fc5b7413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375551} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3815ff-b760-5822-af65-d073a53c800a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "177" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375552} {:answer/id #uuid "64d38e45-914c-5d48-8ce5-2dace689bf18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375553} {:answer/id #uuid "050490e3-df10-578f-985f-c37809a62c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "171" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e52a7d5-7212-505d-a4ad-a9e51e77aaac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "186" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375550} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fbcc315-d216-52b5-a765-5b4e327fcfae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae71496f-04c8-523b-aa38-284e1f4942ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-123235" :question/problem "Let $R=[2,5]\\times [0,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=24-2x-3y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={2,5},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2,3,4},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/017b40453041b9485436e21f4966e530b925f6989795283e664692265ca2f814.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13})+{M}_{14}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{14}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=3$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =3\\cdot {M}_{11}+3\\cdot {M}_{12}+3\\cdot {M}_{13}+3\\cdot {M}_{14} \\\\  & =3({M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}+{M}_{14}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=24-2x-3y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=24-2x-3y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6ceecc26bb9800a2884202b05144dcecbf988bea7953605e4b33e485a4cb1865.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/f9ec123fe8048f8463c845527162a3f28ecc3a597f7f57717dde88909f363906.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(2,0)=20 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(2,1)=17 \\\\ {M}_{13} & =f(2,2)=14 \\\\ {M}_{14} & =f(2,3)=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =3(20+17+14+11) \\\\  & =186.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c02c871-62f2-55c0-b381-2cd56c9cb1a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375557} {:answer/id #uuid "360b5897-c57f-543d-aff4-c3a8e52e8c0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375558} {:answer/id #uuid "6eec82d3-b895-5702-b09f-128366ece29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375559} {:answer/id #uuid "b489483b-b1db-5c1a-8dff-4640b0067129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375560} {:answer/id #uuid "0664ad93-a440-5935-82ae-e477f8ee1169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c02c871-62f2-55c0-b381-2cd56c9cb1a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375557} :answer-field/id #uuid "314a416b-446e-539e-a86e-c2f330bb0910" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e0bc16e-8bdc-51d1-a48d-64ed29bcd682" :question/math-academy-id "q-123238" :question/problem "Let $R=[2,8]\\times [3,7]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=15-x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={2,5,8},\\,{P}_{2}={3,5,7},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/e216f0629c9213ab54c869e9eede5bf61b717c45aed872ad9791781bf7af180c.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=6$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =6\\cdot {M}_{11}+6\\cdot {M}_{21}+6\\cdot {M}_{12}+6\\cdot {M}_{22} \\\\  & =6({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=15-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=15-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/a6249a828aebb94d1149f0083fee68e026b8c2ff1c32a3ae3b1c0471910a6c68.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/b162af4ffab30303889adc3ec1fae04e1a8cc943826972429643a71c2a2ed454.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(2,3)=10 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(5,3)=7 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(2,5)=8 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(5,5)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =6(10+7+8+5) \\\\  & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89071eb7-32aa-5054-ac9a-bf45b50dc4ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150715} {:answer/id #uuid "f08f2d03-41d0-5cbc-8927-6c288db88a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150716} {:answer/id #uuid "102be2cf-56dd-50c0-bbe4-ce9976bb3983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150717} {:answer/id #uuid "32addc48-c7e6-5b0c-94a9-359b235268ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150718} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd61a8b-ad3a-54c0-b6ca-d88b60500700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f08f2d03-41d0-5cbc-8927-6c288db88a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150716} :answer-field/id #uuid "57d73b5f-0ee0-53d1-baf6-217b604bd60f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06416db6-a5ef-5504-be7c-78a29bd00e0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75048" :question/problem "Let $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=5-3x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={-1,0,1},\\,{P}_{2}={-1,0,1},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/859d9d6b15dbd08b4c015816e46986268f92a94770df866a562f3e379bae5365.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-3x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-3x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/4c688c7cf529800b8f1ad075dc70784e40a59163330031b375eaad4b1cdd64c9.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6269ba3c4fd853fff89327ab9e6968e2f52439f5c86c0ec5a21dce1620d9ba5d.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(-1,-1)=9 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(0,-1)=6 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(-1,0)=8 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(0,0)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =9+6+8+5 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27592f4a-1bcd-5db1-af0d-b9b9542d6346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375564} {:answer/id #uuid "997b2e7d-0ced-5822-a33e-49172f5b35b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375565} {:answer/id #uuid "d79cfb40-b33d-53ad-a7b7-f0f4448e671f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375566} {:answer/id #uuid "b846a42a-3691-5b96-b345-e77267c251ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375567} {:answer/id #uuid "597e052b-f776-54c4-ab75-a9cb08a58778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d79cfb40-b33d-53ad-a7b7-f0f4448e671f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375566} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bb0afae-b9fb-599a-9aa6-3e816ca0210e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "483dbf42-ff77-50b7-9a68-ddceddaeccbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-75049" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/3cac974f7352243bfbe008ad8af58ae8222709f81f434070383cc759a46747c7.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the maximum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/d7e548ae50bc8c7f0e33e8bb43ba8950788f735af103c4923d50d147995a3bb6.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6aee54ead228fa1f7cda200a25db0d745634dec8194d492d9075da82dde3ce5c.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,1)=4 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,2)=4 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(2,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =3+4+4+5 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6248872-3f8a-5203-926f-2fb1052f687d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375571} {:answer/id #uuid "77f18b8a-bbd4-521a-9956-dee93046b014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375572} {:answer/id #uuid "1be4fd9e-b3a6-57a8-9093-85d1e7698ac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375573} {:answer/id #uuid "f166c7ad-5df3-5940-9b9d-42c16a0da433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375574} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5207a5-0478-57af-aba5-522ec9358c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a5207a5-0478-57af-aba5-522ec9358c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375575} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b575e76-5e4a-5db9-aa86-961783b920bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a23faa4-c961-5120-9549-d6ff324d41c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-75175" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,2]\\times [3,5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=3x+2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={3,4,5},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/e51bb7736f316547ca179513705721f20ea2404a2713c7ae07af3a03616e7373.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ becuase we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for both subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=3x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=3x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/0aa78337b37fe81cbf4b3beba78967c92191f8ca7f9445931f5a4d532f806e53.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/26fba6a927a7d7d097f278fbc2902e056ceb41356377b23f138a1598ac3d22dc.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(2,4)=14 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(2,5)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =14+16 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fa5909-6036-5c58-a3e1-d70d6444288c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "196" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375578} {:answer/id #uuid "65e2bbb0-bb50-5b28-87fe-30d2063504c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375579} {:answer/id #uuid "3db0f7d3-e7cc-59f4-bc8c-57464e7e6e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375580} {:answer/id #uuid "a0625d2c-22fe-58c1-925f-fdf21b6872a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "208" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375581} {:answer/id #uuid "cb44427e-d489-541a-8780-25a58806cd0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0625d2c-22fe-58c1-925f-fdf21b6872a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "208" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375581} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc471930-bebc-5f65-9de7-5db0e9160a03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f07888aa-4e32-526a-8901-15a2e550b7f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75182" :question/problem "Let $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=2x+y+10.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={-2,0,2},\\,{P}_{2}={-2,0,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6a44295e07e8d14dfae78403d4629bde77c522136d5ffec16ace6416db72303e.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{21}+4\\cdot {M}_{12}+4\\cdot {M}_{22} \\\\  & =4({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=2x+y+10$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=2x+y+10$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/bdb4b6095c1b449d8e048f85a0d4d289758597af520ed61f545dbe280560ca15.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/ca1c7e423afd88b0f1b18570bad73897092167ad338dcdbe275aaf451a08b3cb.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(0,0)=10 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,0)=14 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(0,2)=12 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(2,2)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4(10+14+12+16) \\\\  & =208.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f7fe39-9ca2-5c27-8852-96eb58a51f62" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f288f0-7583-5df4-8d84-0bee1c94f0cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375585} {:answer/id #uuid "8fafaa9e-aa93-56f5-ab23-da5400313138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375586} {:answer/id #uuid "2d233b45-4f9f-5144-b08e-c6386705b4fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375587} {:answer/id #uuid "9e06bf7b-90fe-5cf5-ac56-a7340e9836da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375588} {:answer/id #uuid "2a324d08-8dba-59ec-bf44-e3419b53cd5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83f288f0-7583-5df4-8d84-0bee1c94f0cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375585} :answer-field/id #uuid "be60ce49-f2d8-5d01-a18c-4627dc0a9f13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc65c5e4-df36-5a81-baa5-38da10705533" :question/math-academy-id "q-75186" :question/problem "Let $R=[3,5]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={3,4,5},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/ed8a998b543cb951a12599de55004c9220a53ac252a9eea66577475e2b8b6093.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-2$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-2$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/a0033237f985416771125780a3d9b68bdfd8c6c33b9a4b0f2a32fa7af4c59bca.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/26cae8b6e2c69c2c0c66d78eb64c5d02fc500d10a970e54b09f6ee72bc145b74.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(4,2)=26 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(5,2)=35 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(4,3)=41 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(5,3)=50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =26+35+41+50 \\\\  & =152.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cd571d2-a2d6-534d-84dd-a8182f64d1d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Upper Riemann Sum in the Case of a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc6eccab-a2ca-5f1e-90ec-4c377ec191b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375592} {:answer/id #uuid "b4617023-0d07-56f0-b703-9474cd6bdb01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375593} {:answer/id #uuid "32d8731a-aaa3-585b-89db-57bf460e3838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375594} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd67aa3-8151-5e40-9969-6b2899ff83fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375595} {:answer/id #uuid "618e8c24-82d7-5297-a93b-1f1f3fe09667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bd67aa3-8151-5e40-9969-6b2899ff83fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375595} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f8f30aa-422b-5742-a14e-63472ce9b8ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "553db302-0825-590a-892d-d3b98e4e5053" :question/math-academy-id "q-75232" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [2,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={2,3,4},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/b7581f31a2031181aeb31bbdf8a3da6beff69b50ee41d5d73babbe198997fe15.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/dfed66710976869aa4507aea2e171c09ac248d4b8b4ca083972dbd10952af6b7.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/a6366799e116eb35e8d3cb5401f0abc161ad183cd8f634bdaf2ce387f64d6c5c.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=25 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,2)=22 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,3)=20 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(2,3)=17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =25+22+20+17 \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
